-- title:
--   Functions of level N are quotients of integral q-series
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $x$ be a Laurent series with rational coefficients, i.e. an element of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$. Assume $x$ lies in [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L305), which by definition is the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set `divisorExpansions N`, namely by those series of the form `qExpand ℚ d jq` for $d$ a nonzero divisor of $N$; here `jq` is the fixed rational Laurent series playing the role of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$, and `qExpand ℚ d` is the substitution $q \mapsto q^{d}$, which puts the $m$-th coefficient of a series in degree $dm$ and zero in degrees not divisible by $d$. The conclusion is that there exist Laurent series $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}((q))$ with $b \neq 0$ such that $x = \varphi(a)/\varphi(b)$, the quotient being formed in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, where $\varphi =$ [`ModularCurve.coeffMap (Int.castRingHom ℚ)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) is the ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}((q)) \to \mathbb{Q}((q))$ applying the inclusion $\mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{Q}$ to each coefficient. Thus the field of level-$N$ modular functions, in this formal $q$-expansion presentation, is contained in the set of quotients of integral Laurent series.
--
--   This records that the function field of $X_0(N)$, presented through the $q$-expansions $j(q^d)$ for $d \mid N$, lies inside the fraction field of $\mathbb{Z}((q))$ taken inside $\mathbb{Q}((q))$; the content is that the chosen generators have integral coefficients. It supplies the quotient-of-integral-series hypothesis in the bounded-denominators argument, and is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_ne_zero_coeffMap_eq_mul_of_isModPFormFn`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_ne_zero_coeffMap_eq_mul_of_isModPFormFn).
